00:00:46.394 --> 00:00:49.944
So let's take a look and let's dive into
all these different types of structures.

00:00:55.095 --> 00:00:59.175
Okay, so the first thing that we're going
to be looking at is the primary structure.

00:00:59.674 --> 00:01:03.704
So what that is, is when we are
looking at the individual sequence of

00:01:03.704 --> 00:01:06.099
amino acids that are bonded together.

00:01:06.589 --> 00:01:09.649
Now, when we look at one of these
sequences, usually what you're

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:13.289
going to be looking at is different
structures that could be drawn either

00:01:13.289 --> 00:01:17.030
like an expanded structural formula
where it's very linear, kind of 90

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:21.370
degrees, or you might see it where
it's in that zigzag skeletal notation.

00:01:21.725 --> 00:01:23.805
Personally, I like to draw it
this way because it makes it

00:01:23.805 --> 00:01:25.415
a little bit easier to follow.

00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:29.695
So when we're looking at the
backbone of it, the big thing is,

00:01:29.705 --> 00:01:34.335
is that it's a linear chain, meaning
that it's in one continuous line.

00:01:34.875 --> 00:01:39.894
Now when we look at that, you'll see
that it's in the same repeating pattern.

00:01:40.274 --> 00:01:43.394
It always starts at the
end terminal, right?

00:01:43.394 --> 00:01:45.414
So where the amino group is.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.240
Then from there, it's always going to
go from the left to right to where the

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.539
C terminal is or where the carboxylic
acid is at the very end of that chain.

00:01:57.190 --> 00:02:02.790
Now, it always goes from left to right
because each carbon that is in the middle

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.960
of each amino acid is considered to be the
alpha carbon, meaning that has chirality

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:11.770
to it because you have four different
groups around each one of those carbons.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:16.130
So each one of these where you see
an R attached to it, That's where the

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.270
alpha carbon is, like, right above it.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.580
So we have a couple of them
on this particular one.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.280
So now when we look at this, each
amino acid is called a residue,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.349
meaning that each one is unique.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.400
It has potentially these
different unique characteristics.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.779
So you see these R
groups attached to them.

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:37.365
meaning that they have these different
side chains that are attached to each one.

00:02:38.055 --> 00:02:42.325
Now, when we're looking at the sequence,
you'll see that it has this repeating

00:02:42.325 --> 00:02:49.844
pattern, so you'll keep seeing the same
pattern of NCC repeating in the structure.

00:02:50.334 --> 00:02:58.609
So when I see that N Then you got C, C,
so we got N, C, C, N, C, C, and it'll

00:02:58.609 --> 00:03:00.969
keep going as it's bound together.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.609
Now each of those different residues
or amino acids, they're each

00:03:05.619 --> 00:03:08.510
bound together via a peptide bond.

00:03:09.079 --> 00:03:11.980
So you'll see I have these
peptide bonds in green here.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.159
So just to kind of help highlight
them a little bit, it's the

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:16.850
same thing as an amide, right?

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:19.619
So if you think back to organic
chemistry, so that amide was the

00:03:19.619 --> 00:03:23.000
double bond with the nitrogen, you're
looking for the exact same thing.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.749
So if you're an organic chemist and
you're more in favor of that instead

00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:28.399
of biochem, look for the amides.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.450
So being that we have
that repeating pattern.

00:03:31.885 --> 00:03:35.715
The thing about that is that's what
we consider to be the backbone of it.

00:03:36.095 --> 00:03:39.944
So that repeating nitrogen, carbon,
carbon, that is where we talk when

00:03:39.945 --> 00:03:41.695
we say backbone, referring to that.

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:45.635
Now, when we talk about those
amino acids, the way that you can

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:49.764
organize them is we like to describe
