00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:25.000
And the reason for that is because on
the structure, there are amino groups.

00:07:25.465 --> 00:07:28.005
And then there's carbonyl
groups on it, meaning there is

00:07:28.005 --> 00:07:30.195
nitrogen groups and CO groups.

00:07:30.715 --> 00:07:34.695
So what's going to happen is the
amino group is a hydrogen bond donor,

00:07:34.765 --> 00:07:40.325
meaning it has the hydrogen part that's
participating in the hydrogen bond.

00:07:41.115 --> 00:07:45.135
And it's going to hydrogen bond to
the carbonyl on the opposite side.

00:07:45.635 --> 00:07:48.905
So if you kind of remember how we would
label things like that, right, we'd say

00:07:48.905 --> 00:07:52.315
delta negative plus, delta negative plus.

00:07:52.545 --> 00:07:54.834
So we have that acceptor
donor relationship.

00:07:55.565 --> 00:07:59.624
So now that when this occurs is
because that polypeptide that we're

00:07:59.634 --> 00:08:03.915
making as you increase amino acids,
it's going to start folding on itself.

00:08:03.975 --> 00:08:07.585
And the reason it wants to do this
is for stability, is to help protect

00:08:07.615 --> 00:08:09.354
the protein and also serve as.

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:11.360
As help, it's function as well.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:15.030
Every protein has a specific shape,
so it does a specific feature.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.220
It can bond to specific things.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:17.610
It does specific things.

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.140
So stability and function.

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:24.250
So the way that these can look and
look in a couple different ways.

00:08:24.779 --> 00:08:28.040
So the first one is in a helical pattern.

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:31.760
So with this one, if you can
kind of imagine this as like a

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:35.299
kind of like a ribbon that's sort
of like spinning around, right?

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:37.770
So this is spinning, spinning
and spinning and spinning.

00:08:38.425 --> 00:08:42.585
So the alpha helix, the way that
this works is essentially you'll have

00:08:42.585 --> 00:08:46.575
all those hydrogen bonds that are
happening where about every fourth

00:08:46.575 --> 00:08:50.115
amino acid, you'll have a hydrogen
bond that's occurring in this chain.

00:08:50.525 --> 00:08:53.704
So you're going to see all these
little pink, little hydrogen bonds

00:08:54.065 --> 00:08:58.514
within the structure that's going to be
forming between all those amino groups.

00:08:58.965 --> 00:09:04.325
And the carbonyls now, as this is
twisting together, it helps kind of, it

00:09:04.325 --> 00:09:09.094
puts all the R groups on the outside so
that way they can interact and kind of

00:09:09.094 --> 00:09:11.655
even do further interactions around it.

00:09:12.185 --> 00:09:16.094
So it allows it to have this ability
to either connect to different proteins

00:09:16.094 --> 00:09:20.515
maybe, um, or maybe potentially
connect on different sides of it.

00:09:20.525 --> 00:09:23.745
So that way there's different shapes
and different abilities that can form.

00:09:24.375 --> 00:09:25.605
So that was the alpha helix.

00:09:26.235 --> 00:09:30.225
Now, the other option that could
occur is the beta pleated sheet.

00:09:30.605 --> 00:09:34.374
So that the way that this one
works is it's kind of like a zigzag

00:09:34.425 --> 00:09:36.874
pattern that keeps kind of happening.

00:09:36.874 --> 00:09:39.474
So it keeps zigzagging,
then it wraps around.

00:09:39.954 --> 00:09:44.184
And what we're seeing here is we'll
see those hydrogen bond interactions.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:48.609
That are occurring across the
sheet so that in order to help

00:09:48.639 --> 00:09:51.869
bring those together, so it's
protected from anything foreign

00:09:51.879 --> 00:09:53.809
that could eventually take it apart.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.219
So we have these two different
shapes that help do it.

00:09:57.229 --> 00:10:01.800
Now the thing to point out is that this
is between the backbone of it, right?

00:10:01.809 --> 00:10:04.290
It's not our groups that
are talking to each other.

00:10:04.665 --> 00:10:10.845
It's only the pieces of the backbone that
are interacting versus our next structure.

00:10:11.025 --> 00:10:15.044
So in our next structure, the
tertiary structure, this is when

00:10:15.045 --> 00:10:18.015
the side of chains are going to
start to interact with each other.

00:10:18.475 --> 00:10:22.864
And the big idea here is that there's
going to be this spatial arrangement of

00:10:22.865 --> 00:10:24.665
the amino acids that are going to happen.

00:10:25.295 --> 00:10:29.255
So once you start kind of having those
spiral patterns, and especially when

00:10:29.255 --> 00:10:31.315
the amino acid gets really, really big.

00:10:31.884 --> 00:10:34.624
Then you're going to have side
chains that can interact with each

00:10:34.624 --> 00:10:39.585
other across hundreds of amino acids
away because it's folding so much.

00:10:39.845 --> 00:10:41.655
And those R groups are polar as well.

00:10:42.144 --> 00:10:46.854
So what they can do is they can do all
kinds of non covalent interactions, a.

00:10:46.874 --> 00:10:47.304
k.

00:10:47.304 --> 00:10:47.314
a.

00:10:47.314 --> 00:10:50.474
intermolecular forces, where
